alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Sieg (river)</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Sieg is a river in western Germany, about 155 km long, rising in the Rothaar Mountains and flowing west through the Siegerland to join the Rhine near Bonn.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/siege-capability"><span class="enc-name">Siege capability</span><span class="enc-type">Historical factor</span><span class="enc-snip">Siege capability refers to the recurring pattern in which an empire's ability to capture fortified cities determined how far it could expand.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/siege-engine"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/siege-engine.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Siege engine</span><span class="enc-type">Term</span><span class="enc-snip">A siege engine is a device designed to break or bypass fortifications, such as a battering ram, siege tower, catapult or trebuchet.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/siege-of-acre-1189-1191"><span class="enc-name">Siege of Acre (1189–1191)</span><span class="enc-type">Siege · 1189–1191</span><span class="enc-snip">The Siege of Acre (1189–1191) was a long siege of the port city of Acre during the Third Crusade.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/siege-of-akhulgo"><span class="enc-name">Siege of Akhulgo</span><span class="enc-type">Siege · 1839</span><span class="enc-snip">The siege of Akhulgo was a Russian siege of Imam Shamil's mountain fortress in Dagestan in the summer of 1839.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/siege-of-alamut"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/siege-of-alamut.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Siege of Alamut</span><span class="enc-type">Siege · 1256</span><span class="enc-snip">The Siege of Alamut was the capture of the mountain fortress of the Nizari Ismailis in northern Iran by Mongol forces under Hulagu in 1256.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/siege-of-amida-359"><span class="enc-name">Siege of Amida (359)</span><span class="enc-type">Siege · 359 CE</span><span class="enc-snip">The Siege of Amida in 359 CE was the capture of the Roman city of Amida, modern Diyarbakir, by the Sasanian king Shapur II.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/siege-of-antioch-256"><span class="enc-name">Siege of Antioch (256)</span><span class="enc-type">Siege · 256 CE</span><span class="enc-snip">The Siege of Antioch in 256 CE was the capture of the great Roman city of Antioch by the Sasanian king Shapur I.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/siege-of-baghdad-1258"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/siege-of-baghdad-1258.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Siege of Baghdad (1258)</span><span class="enc-type">Siege · 1258</span><span class="enc-snip">The Siege of Baghdad in 1258 was the capture and sack of the Abbasid capital by the Mongol army of Hulagu.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/siege-of-basra-1775-1776"><span class="enc-name">Siege of Basra (1775–1776)</span><span class="enc-type">Siege · 1775–1776</span><span class="enc-snip">The Siege of Basra (1775–1776) was the capture of the Ottoman-held port city of Basra by the forces of the Zand Empire under Karim Khan.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/siege-of-belgrade-1521"><span class="enc-name">Siege of Belgrade (1521)</span><span class="enc-type">Siege · 1521</span><span class="enc-snip">The Siege of Belgrade in 1521 was the capture of the Hungarian fortress city by the Ottoman sultan Suleiman the Magnificent.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/siege-of-bursa"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/imag